>This is a petty point but important if one is to understand statistics.

>If the probability is 1 in 4000 then the chance of it happening after
>4000 times is EXACTLY 50%. In other words, you are on top of the bell curve
>with half of the occurances before 4000 tries and half after 4000.

This is incorrect.  Consider, for example, a 1 in 2 chance.  The probability
of success in the first two tries is 3/4 (e.g. getting at least one head in
two flips of a coin).  In general, if there is a 1 in n chance of success, 
then the probability of at least one success after m tries (m need not 
equal n) is 1-(1-1/n)^m.  One can easily derive this by considering the
probability of *no* successes in the first m tries (hence the "1-").

Applying this formula to the current problem yields a probability of about 
.63 of finding a WoW on the first level, under your assumptions.

By the way, it *is* true that the _average (or expected) number of trials
until the first success_ is exactly 4000 (or n, in the general case). This
involves computing an infinite sum.

